shirudo: kernel-side items to resolve in future
================================================

These are limitations in the upstream kernel and libbpf that shirudo's
design bumps into. None are blockers for the MVP -- each has a userspace
workaround noted below -- but resolving them upstream would let shirudo
tighten its trust model and remove workarounds. File paths below refer to
the pinned tree under third_party/bpf-next/ (currently 523d2f42b406).


1. gen_loader cannot attach the program it loads
------------------------------------------------
tools/lib/bpf/gen_loader.c

The signed SYSCALL loader produced by bpf_object__gen_loader() loads the
inner BPF program in kernel context (kernel == true, so it bypasses
shirudo's lsm/bpf gate) and resolves the program's attach_btf_id
(emit_find_attach_target), but it does NOT emit the BPF_LINK_CREATE /
BPF_RAW_TRACEPOINT_OPEN that actually activates the program. There is no
bpf_gen__link_create(); the public ops are map_create, map_update_elem,
map_freeze, prog_load, load_btf, record_attach_target, record_extern,
record_relo_core. The canonical light-skeleton attach
(bpftool gen.c:codegen_attach_detach -> skel_raw_tracepoint_open(NULL,
prog_fd)) runs in USERSPACE after the loader returns.

Consequence for shirudo: a signed upatch can be loaded from kernel
context but not attached from kernel context. shirudo therefore creates
the link from userspace, which forces the lsm/bpf policy to permit
BPF_LINK_CREATE / BPF_RAW_TRACEPOINT_OPEN from userspace. That is weaker
than the ideal "no mutating bpf() from userspace at all" -- it is
acceptable only because the BPF_PROG_LOAD signature gate already ensures
every non-readonly program in the kernel is either signed or pre-existing
(see threat-model rationale for allowing userspace attach).

Desired: a way for the signed loader to attach the program it just loaded,
from kernel context (e.g. bpf_gen__emit_link_create, or a load-and-attach
flag). Then shirudo could deny BPF_LINK_CREATE / BPF_RAW_TRACEPOINT_OPEN
from userspace again.

Workaround in shirudo today: userspace reads prog_fd from the loader ctx
and issues the attach itself; lsm/bpf permits it.


2. BPF_OBJ_PIN is not callable from a SYSCALL loader (kernel context)
---------------------------------------------------------------------
kernel/bpf/syscall.c  BPF_CALL_3(bpf_sys_bpf, ...) allowlist (~line 6457)

bpf_sys_bpf() -- the helper a SYSCALL/loader program uses to call back into
bpf() from kernel context -- whitelists only:
  MAP_CREATE, MAP_DELETE_ELEM, MAP_UPDATE_ELEM, MAP_FREEZE,
  MAP_GET_FD_BY_ID, PROG_LOAD, BTF_LOAD, LINK_CREATE, RAW_TRACEPOINT_OPEN.
Everything else returns -EINVAL. BPF_OBJ_PIN is absent.

Consequence for shirudo: even if item 1 were solved and the loader could
attach, it still could not pin the resulting link to bpffs from kernel
context. Pinning must happen in userspace.

Desired: allow BPF_OBJ_PIN (and ideally BPF_OBJ_GET) from bpf_sys_bpf so a
signed loader can pin what it attaches, fully in kernel context.

Workaround in shirudo today: userspace pins the link
(/var/lib/shirudo/bpffs/upatches/<name>/link); lsm/bpf permits BPF_OBJ_PIN.


3. Loader ctx has no link_fd[] slot
-----------------------------------
tools/lib/bpf/skel_internal.h  (struct bpf_loader_ctx, bpf_map_desc,
bpf_prog_desc)

The loader writes results back to its ctx: map_fd into bpf_map_desc[],
prog_fd into bpf_prog_desc[]. There is no bpf_link_desc[]. If items 1+2
were solved and the loader created+pinned links itself, there would still
be no channel to surface the link fd to userspace.

Desired: a bpf_link_desc[] in the ctx layout, populated by the loader.

Workaround in shirudo today: not needed yet (userspace creates the link,
so it already holds the fd). Becomes relevant only if items 1+2 land.


4. bpf_map->sha is populated lazily; skipping the trigger fails silently
------------------------------------------------------------------------
kernel/bpf/verifier.c (emit_signature_match read side),
kernel/bpf/syscall.c (info.hash compute), and the gen_loader runtime guard.

The "exclusive map" signing flow puts a runtime guard in the loader that
compares SHA256(map_value), baked into the loader insns at sign time,
against bpf_map->sha read at run time. bpf_map->sha is computed lazily:
it is only filled in when userspace calls BPF_OBJ_GET_INFO_BY_FD with
bpf_map_info.hash / .hash_size set, AFTER BPF_MAP_FREEZE and BEFORE
BPF_PROG_LOAD. Skip that one call and the loader exits -EINVAL at runtime
with an empty verifier log and no kernel-context bpf() call ever made --
a hard-to-diagnose failure (cost us a debugging session; see commit
"Seal __loader.map hash via BPF_OBJ_GET_INFO_BY_FD").

Desired: compute the map hash at BPF_MAP_FREEZE time, or document the
ordering requirement in the uapi, or have the loader runtime guard return
a distinguishable error.

Workaround in shirudo today: builtin-upatch.c:loader_map_seal_hash()
issues the OBJ_GET_INFO_BY_FD call in the right place.


5. Signed loaders cannot run from kernel context (no userspace)
---------------------------------------------------------------
Per KP Singh's commit fb2b0e290147 ("libbpf: Update light skeleton for
signing"): "loading signed programs prebuilt into the kernel are not
currently supported. These can [be] supported by enabling
BPF_OBJ_GET_INFO_BY_ID to be called from the kernel."

Consequence for shirudo: a future initramfs-stage `shirudo init` (to close
the boot-time enforcement gap, threat T5) that wanted to load signed
upatches before userspace is fully up would hit this -- the info-by-id
hashing path (item 4's trigger) is userspace-only.

Desired: enable the BPF_OBJ_GET_INFO_BY_ID map-hash path from kernel
context, so signed loaders can be driven from a kernel/early-boot context.

Workaround in shirudo today: none needed -- MVP init runs from userspace
under sshd. Relevant only when the initramfs-stage init lands.


6. A signed loader cannot detach/unpin, so unload runs in userspace
-------------------------------------------------------------------
kernel/bpf/syscall.c  BPF_CALL_3(bpf_sys_bpf, ...) allowlist (~line 6457)

The bpf_sys_bpf() allowlist (see item 2) has no BPF_LINK_DETACH,
BPF_OBJ_GET, or BPF_OBJ_PIN. So a signed SYSCALL program cannot, from
kernel context: open a pinned link by path (OBJ_GET), detach it
(LINK_DETACH), or unpin it. The PLAN.md design for `upatch unload`
("a signed BPF program issues BPF_LINK_DETACH + BPF_OBJ_UNPIN from
kernel context, cross-checking payload_sha256") is therefore not
implementable on the current kernel.

Consequence for shirudo: `upatch unload` verifies the directive's CMS
signature via BPF_PROG_LOAD (the authorization gate, same signed path as
load), but performs the actual detach in USERSPACE -- it VFS-unlinks the
pinned link (refcount drop detaches the program). bpf2's inode_unlink
permits this for shirudo's own exe identity (item: inode_unlink identity)
and denies every other caller, so the pin protection holds.

Two gaps remain because the detach isn't the signed BPF acting on its
own baked-in, hash-bound params:
  - Target binding: the unloaded name comes from the directive header /
    metadata, which are not signature-covered. A root attacker holding
    ANY validly-signed unload directive could repoint it to a different
    name. (Unsigned unload is still prevented -- the signature must
    verify -- but target-specificity is not cryptographically enforced.)
  - payload_mismatch: confirming the loaded upatch's payload_sha256
    matches the directive IS now enforced -- but in userspace, not by
    the signed BPF. shirudo reads the security.bpf.shirudo.payload
    xattr on the pin directory (set at load, made unforgeable by bpf2's
    inode_setxattr guard; see item 7) and constant-time compares it to
    the directive's signed expected payload before detaching. Moving
    this comparison + the delete into the signed unload program (so it
    is the signed BPF acting on its own hash-bound params) needs the
    in-kernel ops below.

Desired: allow BPF_LINK_DETACH + BPF_OBJ_GET (and OBJ_PIN, item 2) from
bpf_sys_bpf so the signed unload program enforces the name/payload it was
signed with, in kernel context. Then userspace would not perform the
detach, the target binding would be cryptographic, and the
payload_mismatch compare would move out of userspace.

Workaround in shirudo today: userspace detach gated by signature
verification + bpf2 exe-identity inode_unlink; userspace payload_mismatch
compare against the bpf2-protected pin xattr (item 7); documented
residual on target binding.


7. bpffs supported no extended attributes (RESOLVED by a local patch)
---------------------------------------------------------------------
kernel/bpf/inode.c

Status: implemented as a local patch to this tree (kernel/bpf/inode.c);
not yet upstream. shirudo's option-C payload storage depends on it, so
the patch must ship with / precede the kernel shirudo targets.

Before the patch, bpffs inodes carried no xattrs: the inode_operations
were empty stubs and the superblock installed no s_xattr handler array,
so the VFS short-circuited setxattr/getxattr to -EOPNOTSUPP
(fs/xattr.c xattr_resolve_name). There was nowhere to attach per-pin
metadata to a pinned link/map/prog or to the mount root.

The patch gives bpffs a real fs-private inode (struct bpf_fs_inode
wrapping struct inode, with a lazily-allocated simple_xattrs store), a
kmem_cache with alloc_inode/free_inode, the trusted/security/user
xattr_handlers backed by simple_xattr_get/set, sb->s_xattr, and
.listxattr on the inode ops. The store is in-memory and non-persistent
(lost on reboot), which is exactly what shirudo wants -- it re-applies
on init. The security.bpf. xattr namespace and the bpf_get_dentry_xattr
/ bpf_set_dentry_xattr kfuncs already existed (fs/bpf_fs_kfuncs.c); only
the bpffs backing store was missing.

How shirudo uses it: every loaded upatch's payload_sha256 is recorded
as the security.bpf.shirudo.payload xattr on its pin directory
(/var/lib/shirudo/bpffs/upatches/<name>/), the ground truth for the
unload payload_mismatch check. Immutability against a root attacker
comes from bpf2's inode_setxattr / inode_removexattr hooks (exe-identity
gated over the bpffs superblock), NOT from the xattr store itself --
bpffs xattrs are settable by any CAP_SYS_ADMIN caller otherwise.

Desired: upstream the patch. Until then it is carried locally.


8. libbpf gen_loader cannot bind a pre-existing (pinned) map in the
   signed instruction stream
-------------------------------------------------------------------
tools/lib/bpf/gen_loader.c (bpf_gen__map_create emits BPF_MAP_CREATE for
every tracked map), tools/lib/bpf/libbpf.c (bpf_object__create_maps; the
only reuse path, bpf_object__reuse_map, opens the pin via bpf_obj_get in
userspace at load time)

A gen_loader-produced SYSCALL loader has deterministic, signable insns
precisely because every map reference is an fd_array INDEX resolved at
run time, not a raw fd. But the loader CREATES every non-data map fresh
at run time; there is no public-API way to say "this map already exists
-- bind the fd I provide via the ctx and skip creation". The reuse path
opens the pin with bpf_obj_get in userspace, which does not apply on the
operator at build time (gen_loader path, no target pin). The loader
ctx's bpf_map_desc.map_fd is an output (the loader writes the created fd
there); only max_entries is read back as input.

Consequence for shirudo: a single shared, pinned upatches_registry map
written and compared by signed in-kernel BPF (an earlier PLAN.md design)
is not implementable on stock libbpf -- the signed registry-write /
unload program cannot address the shared map. Realizing it would require
a libbpf fork (gen_loader.c + libbpf.c), breaking the "vendor libbpf
as-is" posture and adding a rebase cost.

shirudo's decision: do NOT carry a libbpf fork. The payload_sha256 is
stored as a per-pin bpffs xattr instead (item 7), which needs neither a
shared map nor a signed in-kernel writer.

Desired (only if the shared-map design is ever revived): a libbpf
gen_loader option for "provided maps" -- a map whose fd is supplied
through the loader ctx (bpf_map_desc.map_fd as input), for which the
loader skips MAP_CREATE. Combined with the bpf_sys_bpf detach ops from
item 6, shirudo could then keep one shared registry and move the whole
load-time write and unload-time compare+delete into signed kernel
context.

Workaround in shirudo today: per-pin bpffs payload xattr (item 7); no
shared registry map, no libbpf fork.


9. Scoping BPF signature verification to one keyring -- RESOLVED (userspace)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
kernel/bpf/syscall.c bpf_prog_verify_signature() ->
bpf_lookup_user_key(attr->keyring_id, 0) -> lookup_user_key()

Originally feared a kernel limitation: shirudo passed attr.keyring_id =
KEY_SPEC_USER_KEYRING (-4), whose keyring_search recurses through the
whole user keyring (_uid.0), so a root attacker could link their own
cert anywhere in _uid.0 and get BPF they signed accepted -- and a stale
comment claimed a positive serial fails with EINVAL.

Not a kernel limitation. lookup_user_key()'s positive-serial path only
returns EINVAL for id < 1; a valid serial goes through key_lookup() +
possession resolution. The loading task possesses the sealed shirudo
keyring (a subkeyring of _uid.0), so passing that keyring's serial as
attr.keyring_id works: verification is then confined to the immutable
shirudo keyring. shirudo now does this (builtin-upatch.c resolves the
serial via shirudo_keyring_find at load time). Verified: a upatch signed
by a foreign cert planted directly in _uid.0 is rejected (ENOKEY) where
the -4 search would have accepted it.

Residual (separate vector): a root attacker could unlink the real
"shirudo" keyring from _uid.0 (or link a fake same-named keyring, which
displaces the real one in _uid.0's by-name index), so find-by-name
resolves to the attacker's keyring.

Mitigated in userspace (does NOT need a kernel change): init pins the
keyring serial to /var/lib/shirudo/keyring.serial (under the seal), and
every upatch load refuses with category "tampered" unless the
resolved-by-name serial still equals the pin. That converts the
substitution from a silent bypass (attacker's cert accepted) into a
fail-closed refusal: the attacker can DoS by removing the keyring but
cannot get BPF they signed loaded. Same trust level as the rest of the
sealed tree (inherits residual #12, the CAP_SYS_RESOURCE exe-spoof).
Verified: swap -> find-by-name returns the fake serial -> load refuses
"keyring serial mismatch (substituted keyring)"; status shows MISMATCH.

A complete (not fail-closed-only) fix needs new kernel code, NOT just a
seal extension. The natural hook, lsm/key_permission, IS attachable from
BPF LSM (not in bpf_lsm_disabled_hooks; only key_getsecurity is) and the
unlink path even checks KEY_NEED_UNLINK on the target key itself
(security/keys/keyctl.c keyctl_keyring_unlink), so the hook would see the
shirudo keyring. BUT its first arg is key_ref_t == an opaque
struct __key_reference_with_attributes * with the possession bit OR'd
into the low pointer bits (include/linux/key.h); BPF gets it as a
PTR_TO_BTF_ID of a type with no members and cannot mask+recast it to
struct key *, and there is no kfunc converting key_ref_t -> struct key *
(the existing key kfuncs -- bpf_lookup_user_key/bpf_lookup_system_key/
bpf_key_put -- take serials / struct bpf_key, not a hook's key_ref_t).
So the hook cannot identify "this is the shirudo keyring". Closing it
fully wants a new kfunc (e.g. bpf_key_ref_to_key(), KF_TRUSTED_ARGS) so
the hook can CO-RE-read key->serial/description and deny by exe-identity;
the displace-by-link half also needs gating link into _uid.0. Until then
the userspace serial pin is the mitigation. The cert-plant hole (the
common one) is closed by the serial scoping above.
